+
+# Meme Mutation, Distribution, Selection, Decay, and Provenance Runtime
+
+## Purpose
+
+Define how a created memetic artifact moves through actual social and communications networks, acquires variants, competes for attention, changes meaning, disappears, returns, and becomes evidence or folklore.
+
+The runtime must produce cultural history rather than a global virality counter.
+
+# 1. Authoritative Objects
+
+```rust
+struct MemeArtifact {
+    meme_id: MemeId,
+    genome_ref: MemeGenomeRef,
+    creator: AgentOrGroupId,
+    created_tick: ChronicleTick,
+    origin_location: EntityId,
+    initial_audience: ConstituencyId,
+    publication_scope: DisclosureScope,
+    media_refs: Vec<MediaRef>,
+    provenance: ProvenanceRecord,
+}
+
+struct MemeVariant {
+    variant_id: MemeVariantId,
+    parent_ids: Vec<MemeVariantId>,
+    artifact_id: MemeId,
+    mutation_ops: Vec<MutationOp>,
+    expression: ExpressionRef,
+    meaning_frames: Vec<WeightedMeaning>,
+    created_by: AgentOrGroupId,
+    created_tick: ChronicleTick,
+    location_id: EntityId,
+}
+
+struct MemeExposure {
+    exposure_id: ExposureId,
+    variant_id: MemeVariantId,
+    recipient: AgentOrGroupId,
+    channel_id: ChannelId,
+    emitted_tick: ChronicleTick,
+    received_tick: ChronicleTick,
+    attention_quality: f32,
+    comprehension: ConfidenceBand,
+    response: ExposureResponse,
+}
+```
+
+# 2. Distribution Graph
+
+Memes move only through valid edges.
+
+Channels include:
+
+- face-to-face speech;
+- household messages;
+- workplace shifts;
+- public mesh networks;
+- local broadcasts;
+- commercial media;
+- performances;
+- schools;
+- religious gatherings;
+- migration;
+- trade;
+- military service;
+- graffiti and durable objects;
+- machine protocols;
+- Atlas communications;
+- seed-ship archives;
+- alien translation interfaces.
+
+Every edge declares:
+
+- bandwidth;
+- latency;
+- access;
+- censorship;
+- cost;
+- audience composition;
+- persistence;
+- discoverability;
+- content constraints;
+- translation path.
+
+Disconnected systems cannot share variants until a carrier or communication path exists.
+
+# 3. Exposure Is Not Adoption
+
+A recipient may:
+
+```text
+not notice
+notice but not understand
+understand and dislike
+understand and forget
+share unchanged
+share with mutation
+quote privately
+use as a response
+commercialize
+report
+suppress
+archive
+reclaim
+weaponize
+```
+
+Adoption depends on:
+
+- relevance;
+- relationship to sender;
+- cultural voice fit;
+- emotional state;
+- status and prestige;
+- usefulness;
+- ease of reproduction;
+- risk;
+- novelty;
+- repetition;
+- competing artifacts;
+- whether the recipient wants to affiliate with the source group.
+
+# 4. Mutation Operators
+
+The runtime supports traceable operators.
+
+## Lexical
+
+- substitute a person, place, institution, tool, or event;
+- shorten;
+- expand;
+- translate;
+- alter register;
+- deliberately mistranslate;
+- turn technical language into slang;
+- replace a proper name with a role.
+
+## Visual
+
+- crop;
+- juxtapose;
+- redraw;
+- alter pose;
+- recolor according to local symbolism;
+- replace background;
+- simplify into iconography;
+- add or remove institutional branding.
+
+## Musical and Audio
+
+- transpose;
+- alter rhythm;
+- change instrumentation;
+- sample a phrase;
+- call-and-response;
+- stretch or compress timing;
+- imitate a machine or environmental sound;
+- convert speech cadence into melody.
+
+## Social
+
+- change the target;
+- reverse admiration and ridicule;
+- move from private to public;
+- reclaim an insult;
+- turn coping humor into propaganda;
+- commercialize;
+- ritualize;
+- attach to a new event;
+- merge with an older local story.
+
+## Material
+
+- stencil onto a vehicle;
+- turn into clothing;
+- alter an architectural feature;
+- add to a tool;
+- encode in a route procedure;
+- convert into a game or gesture.
+
+Every operator preserves parentage, even when characters no longer know it.
+
+# 5. Mutation Pressure
+
+Mutation probability rises when:
+
+- the meme crosses language or species boundaries;
+- the medium changes;
+- the audience lacks the original referent;
+- political interests benefit from reinterpretation;
+- a new event resembles the old structure;
+- youth adopt an older form;
+- censorship requires coded speech;
+- commercial actors need generic reuse;
+- memory becomes incomplete;
+- the source community deliberately reclaims control.
+
+Mutation probability falls when:
+
+- the artifact is technically fixed;
+- provenance is institutionally protected;
+- ritual requires exact repetition;
+- the audience treats alteration as disrespect;
+- automated systems verify exact hashes;
+- a private group actively contains the joke.
+
+# 6. Meaning Drift
+
+The system separates expression from interpreted meaning.
+
+A single variant may mean:
+
+- affectionate teasing to one household;
+- political criticism to workers;
+- aspirational branding to a company;
+- evidence of disrespect to an elder group;
+- an incomprehensible old phrase to children;
+- a sacred remembrance to survivors.
+
+Meaning state is constituency-specific.
+
+```rust
+struct MemeMeaningState {
+    variant_id: MemeVariantId,
+    constituency_id: ConstituencyId,
+    awareness: f32,
+    usage: f32,
+    identification: f32,
+    affect: AffectVector,
+    frames: Vec<WeightedMeaning>,
+    source_attribution: Vec<WeightedSourceClaim>,
+    taboo_state: TabooState,
+}
+```
+
+# 7. Cultural Selection
+
+Selection is not one fitness score. The runtime evaluates several dimensions:
+
+```text
+recognizability
+reusability
+emotional release
+identity value
+prestige
+power support
+risk
+cost of reproduction
+network reach
+translation resilience
+material durability
+novelty
+fatigue
+```
+
+A low-reach workplace phrase may persist for centuries because apprenticeship reproduces it. A globally promoted corporate format may vanish in weeks because nobody identifies with it.
+
+# 8. Fatigue and Saturation
+
+Every audience maintains fatigue by:
+
+- variant family;
+- frame;
+- referent;
+- medium;
+- creator;
+- social function.
+
+Consequences of fatigue:
+
+- reduced attention;
+- ironic overuse;
+- anti-memes;
+- generational rejection;
+- migration to a new format;
+- institutional abandonment;
+- nostalgia after absence.
+
+Symthaea’s presentation layer should suppress overexposure before the culture itself necessarily stops using the meme.
+
+# 9. Decay
+
+Memes decay when:
+
+- source groups disperse;
+- channels close;
+- the referent loses relevance;
+- reproduction becomes costly;
+- language changes;
+- competing formats dominate;
+- institutions suppress them;
+- embarrassment leads to deliberate forgetting;
+- archives are lost;
+- the joke depends on private knowledge no longer transmitted.
+
+Decay states:
+
+```text
+active
+niche
+fatigued
+dormant
+archival
+misremembered
+ritualized
+commercial fossil
+extinct
+```
+
+Extinct in active culture does not mean deleted from historical evidence.
+
+# 10. Revival
+
+Revival may follow:
+
+- anniversary;
+- political recurrence;
+- discovery of an old recording;
+- retro fashion;
+- descendants reclaiming a founder joke;
+- a new event fitting the old frame;
+- contact with an isolated settlement where the meme remained active;
+- corporate repackaging;
+- scholarly or archaeological interest.
+
+A revival may preserve, invert, sanitize, radicalize, or misunderstand the original meaning.
+
+# 11. Suppression and Censorship
+
+Institutions may:
+
+- remove content;
+- punish creators;
+- throttle networks;
+- flood channels with alternatives;
+- purchase or co-opt the format;
+- classify the source event;
+- alter archives;
+- criminalize symbols;
+- pressure employers or households;
+- use automated filters.
+
+Suppression creates costs and traces:
+
+- missing posts;
+- arrests or disciplinary records;
+- coded variants;
+- diaspora preservation;
+- Streisand effects;
+- false provenance;
+- chilling effects;
+- migration to material media.
+
+The runtime does not assume suppression makes a meme stronger. Many are successfully erased from public circulation.
+
+# 12. Commercialization
+
+Commercial actors can:
+
+- sponsor creators;
+- license a format;
+- sell merchandise;
+- remove risky specificity;
+- attach the meme to products;
+- manufacture apparent grassroots adoption;
+- pay for distribution;
+- compensate or exploit source communities.
+
+Commercialization may increase reach while reducing identification, or may give a source community resources and prestige.
+
+# 13. Propaganda Conversion
+
+A joke can become propaganda when actors deliberately use its compression and familiarity to shape political belief.
+
+Required state:
+
+- sponsor;
+- intended claim;
+- target audience;
+- amplification resources;
+- provenance concealment;
+- factual relationship;
+- dehumanization or harassment risk;
+- counter-speech;
+- measurable institutional consequences.
+
+The cultural runtime records this transformation rather than creating a separate unrelated propaganda artifact.
+
+# 14. Light Delay and Atlas Networks
+
+Interplanetary and interstellar memes carry dates and knowledge age.
+
+A system may receive:
+
+- a meme about an official who has been dead for years;
+- a response after the original political crisis ended;
+- a format whose source civilization no longer exists;
+- several variants arriving through different routes in the wrong historical order.
+
+Atlas corridors reduce latency only while operational. Route outages create divergent local meme lineages.
+
+# 15. Multiplayer Authority
+
+The server or authoritative worldline service owns:
+
+- artifact creation;
+- variant identity;
+- exposure events;
+- network delivery;
+- mutation seed;
+- provenance lineage;
+- moderation and content settings.
+
+Clients may locally suppress presentation according to settings, but cannot create contradictory public culture without an authoritative player action.
+
+Player-created media enters through validated publication actions with:
+
+- creator identity;
+- content hash;
+- scope;
+- moderation state;
+- licensing;
+- worldline binding.
+
+# 16. Provenance Graph
+
+Every variant belongs to a directed acyclic lineage graph within a worldline.
+
+Merge variants may have several parents.
+
+Required invariants:
+
+- no variant is its own ancestor;
+- parent creation precedes child creation;
+- required source claims were available to the creator;
+- cross-worldline references require explicit artifact import or Chronicle-only comparison;
+- deletion from presentation does not silently delete provenance;
+- archival uncertainty is represented rather than resolved by fiat.
+
+# 17. Historical Reconstruction
+
+Characters may investigate meme origins through:
+
+- interviews;
+- old messages;
+- hashes;
+- graffiti layers;
+- recordings;
+- tool provenance;
+- publication archives;
+- machine memory;
+- translation comparison;
+- contradictory oral histories.
+
+The authoritative runtime may know the exact lineage while the player-facing history remains uncertain.
+
+# 18. Performance and Aggregation
+
+Distant regions do not simulate every exposure individually.
+
+Aggregation tiers:
+
+## Embodied
+
+Specific people, messages, performances, and objects.
+
+## Social Network
+
+Group-level exposure with promoted individuals for major adoption or conflict.
+
+## Historical
+
+Variant-family prevalence, meaning, institutions, suppression, and durable artifacts.
+
+## Archival
+
+Lineage summaries, surviving records, extinct forms, and revival predicates.
+
+Promotion restores specific artifacts from deterministic records without inventing false witnesses.
+
+# 19. Validation
+
+Required tests:
+
+- local spread without global leakage;
+- light-delay delivery order;
+- isolated seed-world divergence;
+- private joke containment;
+- multi-parent remix lineage;
+- fatigue and anti-meme formation;
+- corporate amplification without automatic affection;
+- censorship with and without successful suppression;
+- source-community reclamation;
+- extinct-to-revival transition;
+- multiplayer host migration;
+- archival reconstruction with disputed attribution;
+- 100-year aggregate replay stability.
+
+## Runtime Maxim
+
+> **A meme becomes endless not because it is generated forever, but because people keep finding new uses for a remembered form as the world around it changes.**
